WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.217 --> 00:00:10.218
Transistors today

00:00:10.218 --> 00:00:11.678
can be made smaller and smaller

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:14.014
Much of the credit goes to

00:00:14.014 --> 00:00:15.348
this man

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:16.516
in the past two episodes, we mentioned

00:00:16.516 --> 00:00:18.268
how Intel and several Japanese companies

00:00:18.268 --> 00:00:20.812
popularized CPUs and DRAMs

00:00:20.812 --> 00:00:22.480
created the personal computer market

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.482
and ushered in the era of chips

00:00:24.482 --> 00:00:25.817
As demand for

00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:27.485
more efficient IC grew 

00:00:27.485 --> 00:00:30.196
scientists were troubled by the question:

00:00:30.196 --> 00:00:32.115
how do we make ICs even smaller?

00:00:32.115 --> 00:00:33.116
It seemed they had suddenly hit

00:00:33.116 --> 00:00:35.410
the limits of transistor development

00:00:36.077 --> 00:00:37.037
At that moment

00:00:37.037 --> 00:00:38.830
an engineer of Texas Instruments 

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:40.290
offered an innovative solution

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:42.709
He pioneered photolithography

00:00:42.751 --> 00:00:44.210
in semiconductor industry

00:00:44.252 --> 00:00:45.712
Thus began the race

00:00:45.712 --> 00:00:47.213
to chase Moore’s Law

00:00:47.213 --> 00:00:48.506
by photolithography

00:00:48.506 --> 00:00:49.215
till now

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:50.800
Extreme ultraviolet lithography

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:51.968
EUVL

00:00:51.968 --> 00:00:54.846
has cemented ASML, the leading EUV machine manufacturer,

00:00:54.846 --> 00:00:57.390
as a key player in the semiconductor industry

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:59.642
And a ban on EUV equipment sales

00:00:59.642 --> 00:01:00.518
is enough to shake

00:01:00.518 --> 00:01:02.812
China’s entire semiconductor industry

00:01:02.812 --> 00:01:04.272
What on earth is photolithography?

00:01:04.272 --> 00:01:06.608
Why is it crucial to the semiconductor industry?

00:01:06.608 --> 00:01:07.734
Let’s take a look at

00:01:07.734 --> 00:01:09.319
the story of this engineer

00:01:09.319 --> 00:01:11.946
His name is Jay Lathrop

00:01:15.992 --> 00:01:17.327
We mentioned in episode 6

00:01:17.327 --> 00:01:18.787
an TI engineer 

00:01:18.787 --> 00:01:20.038
After Jack Kilby

00:01:20.038 --> 00:01:22.207
Joined TI in 1958

00:01:22.207 --> 00:01:25.126
it took him only 4 months to create the IC

00:01:25.126 --> 00:01:26.795
He is regarded one of the inventors of the IC

00:01:26.795 --> 00:01:29.380
alongside Robert Noyce of Fairchild Semiconductor

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:30.924
You could say that without them

00:01:30.924 --> 00:01:32.926
there would be no “chips” today

00:01:32.926 --> 00:01:34.511
There was more good news

00:01:34.511 --> 00:01:36.054
for Texas Instruments that year

00:01:36.054 --> 00:01:38.556
Another engineer joined the company 

00:01:38.556 --> 00:01:40.975
and introduced the crucial technology of photolithography

00:01:40.975 --> 00:01:42.143
which helped TI

00:01:42.143 --> 00:01:44.145
rapidly enhance its ability

00:01:44.145 --> 00:01:45.480
to mass-produce ICs

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.982
Let’s take a step back to a year

00:01:47.982 --> 00:01:50.193
before this key figure

00:01:50.235 --> 00:01:52.570
joined TI 

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:54.239
It was 1957

00:01:54.239 --> 00:01:55.448
and Jay Lathrop was still working

00:01:55.448 --> 00:01:58.451
atthe U.S. Army’s Diamond Ordnance Fuze Laboratory

00:01:58.451 --> 00:01:59.994
This lab was responsible for developing

00:01:59.994 --> 00:02:01.412
the proximity fuse

00:02:01.412 --> 00:02:02.831
which allowed mortar shells to detonate

00:02:02.831 --> 00:02:05.708
at  a specific altitude above theirtarget

00:02:05.708 --> 00:02:07.293
for increased destructive effect

00:02:07.293 --> 00:02:09.879
They attempted to design a device containing a capacitor 

00:02:09.879 --> 00:02:12.215
that would allow the falling shell

00:02:12.215 --> 00:02:14.717
to accumulate electric charge

00:02:14.717 --> 00:02:15.969
through electrostatic induction between the air and ground

00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:18.555
triggering the fuse for detonation

00:02:18.555 --> 00:02:21.182
Obviously, the bulky vacuum tubes still widely in use

00:02:21.182 --> 00:02:23.768
were not fit for artillery shells

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:24.769
Naturally they turned to

00:02:24.769 --> 00:02:27.730
the rapidly developing transistors technology

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:30.233
trying to replace vacuum tubes with transistors

00:02:30.567 --> 00:02:31.484
Do you remember

00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:33.778
when we talked about the planar transistor structure

00:02:33.778 --> 00:02:35.280
that Noyce introduced

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.407
and patented in 1959

00:02:37.407 --> 00:02:40.034
which came later than Kirby’s invention?

00:02:40.034 --> 00:02:41.619
At that time, transistors were still

00:02:41.619 --> 00:02:44.080
primarily constructed using a mesa structure

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.791
These transistors were usually made of germanium

00:02:46.791 --> 00:02:48.126
stacking semiconductors layers with impurities

00:02:48.126 --> 00:02:51.212
one on top of the other

00:02:51.212 --> 00:02:51.671
Of course, this wasn’t just 

00:02:51.671 --> 00:02:54.132
a simple process of stacking materials

00:02:54.132 --> 00:02:55.216
like kids building blocks

00:02:55.216 --> 00:02:57.468
Every piece got a specific shape

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:58.761
and every layer of the transistor

00:02:58.761 --> 00:03:00.513
required additional processing

00:03:00.513 --> 00:03:02.557
to be precisely shaped.

00:03:02.557 --> 00:03:05.101
To achieve this, they used wax to protect the material

00:03:05.101 --> 00:03:07.020
exposing only the areas that needed to be etched away

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:08.688
through chemical etching

00:03:08.688 --> 00:03:09.522
This semiconductor processing method

00:03:09.522 --> 00:03:11.441
was conceived by Harry Sello

00:03:11.441 --> 00:03:13.484
in Shockley’s laboratory

00:03:13.484 --> 00:03:15.028
The reason to use wax was

00:03:15.028 --> 00:03:16.321
that hydrofluoric acid which was used for etching

00:03:16.321 --> 00:03:18.448
corrodes both silicon and germanium

00:03:18.448 --> 00:03:20.783
but wax is resistant to it

00:03:20.783 --> 00:03:22.368
When hydrofluoric acid was applied,

00:03:22.368 --> 00:03:24.162
the areas not covered by wax

00:03:24.162 --> 00:03:25.914
were gradually etched

00:03:25.914 --> 00:03:27.874
into the desired shape

00:03:27.874 --> 00:03:30.877
while the wax-covered areas remained intact.

00:03:30.919 --> 00:03:33.671
It’s somewhat similar to how children make sand paintings.

00:03:33.671 --> 00:03:36.382
The entire drawing board is initially covered with stickers

00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:37.425
and only when the stickers are removed

00:03:37.425 --> 00:03:38.718
to expose the adhesive

00:03:38.718 --> 00:03:40.803
can it be coated with colorful sand

00:03:40.803 --> 00:03:42.680
Sello’s method 

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:44.098
was called

00:03:44.098 --> 00:03:46.142
wax masking

00:03:46.142 --> 00:03:49.145
It was indeed a simple and effective method.

00:03:49.145 --> 00:03:51.564
However, as transistors became smaller in size

00:03:51.564 --> 00:03:52.523
and more intricate in design,

00:03:52.523 --> 00:03:54.150
problems began to emerge

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:54.984
Researchers discovered that

00:03:54.984 --> 00:03:57.820
it’s hard to draw precise and intricate graphs with wax

00:03:57.820 --> 00:03:59.239
Moreover,the fluidity of wax

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:01.658
caused distortions at the edge of the graphs

00:04:01.658 --> 00:04:04.744
The smaller the graph, the greater the margin of error

00:04:04.786 --> 00:04:05.995
Jay Lathrop and Jim Null

00:04:05.995 --> 00:04:08.373
of Diamond Labs

00:04:08.373 --> 00:04:11.251
were stuck in their efforts

00:04:11.251 --> 00:04:13.211
How could they solve this problem?

00:04:13.211 --> 00:04:14.504
Surprisingly,

00:04:14.504 --> 00:04:16.589
the answer they found at last

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:17.882
came from a company

00:04:17.882 --> 00:04:19.926
that produced celluloid

00:04:19.926 --> 00:04:22.178
Eastman Kodak

00:04:25.723 --> 00:04:28.309
Before charge-coupled devices (CCDs) and digital cameras

00:04:28.309 --> 00:04:29.769
were invented

00:04:29.769 --> 00:04:32.355
film was the primary way to take photos

00:04:32.355 --> 00:04:35.650
Films will be coated with an emulsion of “silver halide” crystals

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:38.486
silver halides include compounds like silver bromide,

00:04:38.486 --> 00:04:40.029
silver iodide and silver chloride--

00:04:40.029 --> 00:04:42.615
all of which are highly sensitive to light

00:04:42.615 --> 00:04:45.660
When a photo is taken, the shutter opens and closes rapidly,

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:48.454
exposing the film for only a brief moment

00:04:48.454 --> 00:04:49.747
Lights passing through the lens

00:04:49.747 --> 00:04:51.916
induces a redox reaction in the silver ions

00:04:51.916 --> 00:04:54.794
causing them to transform from transparent ions into opaque silver atoms

00:04:54.794 --> 00:04:57.422
However, these silver atoms are extremely small and invisible to the naked eye

00:04:57.422 --> 00:04:59.132
which is why they are referred to as a “latent image.”

00:04:59.132 --> 00:05:01.009
During the film development process

00:05:01.009 --> 00:05:03.094
these silver atoms interact with a developer solution

00:05:03.094 --> 00:05:04.929
catalyzing the reduction of nearby silver ions

00:05:04.929 --> 00:05:06.306
back into metallic silver

00:05:06.306 --> 00:05:08.933
This process darkens the emulsion

00:05:08.975 --> 00:05:11.269
forming a visible black-and-white image

00:05:11.269 --> 00:05:13.938
The unexposed areas, which lack a latent image,

00:05:13.938 --> 00:05:15.523
remain transparent

00:05:15.523 --> 00:05:17.317
Lastly the fixer dissolves

00:05:17.317 --> 00:05:18.109
the unreacted transparent silver halide

00:05:18.109 --> 00:05:20.111
without redox reaction

00:05:20.111 --> 00:05:22.655
leaving behind a negative image on the film

00:05:22.655 --> 00:05:23.573
After that

00:05:23.573 --> 00:05:25.491
a negative-to-positive reversal process occurs

00:05:25.491 --> 00:05:26.951
but let’s skip that for now

00:05:26.951 --> 00:05:28.077
The point here is

00:05:28.077 --> 00:05:29.662
that to make better photographs

00:05:29.662 --> 00:05:30.997
such as those with higher resolution

00:05:30.997 --> 00:05:32.081
or full-color images

00:05:32.081 --> 00:05:33.791
that surpass black-and-white ones

00:05:33.791 --> 00:05:36.210
Kodak invested heavily in developing various photosensitive materials

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:37.920
not just silver halides

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:39.339
The material Lathrop chose

00:05:39.339 --> 00:05:41.215
for use in transistor fabrication

00:05:41.215 --> 00:05:43.301
was a product known as “photoresist”

00:05:43.343 --> 00:05:46.679
Photoresist also undergoes chemical changes

00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:48.556
when exposed to ultraviolet lights

00:05:48.556 --> 00:05:50.099
But the point isn’t to get black

00:05:50.099 --> 00:05:52.477
but to alter its dissolution rate

00:05:52.477 --> 00:05:54.771
The exposed areas harden

00:05:54.771 --> 00:05:57.523
while the unexposed parts remain unchanged

00:05:57.523 --> 00:05:59.150
and can be dissolved by a developer

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:00.818
leaving behind the desired graphs

00:06:00.818 --> 00:06:02.695
So, how can light be controlled to selectively

00:06:02.695 --> 00:06:05.365
expose specific areas of the photoresist?

00:06:05.365 --> 00:06:08.284
They found inspiration in everyday microscopes

00:06:08.284 --> 00:06:09.452
The purpose of a microscope is

00:06:09.452 --> 00:06:12.372
to magnify light reflected from objects using lenses

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:15.291
But what if we reversed the microscope?

00:06:15.291 --> 00:06:16.751
Could we then focus light

00:06:16.793 --> 00:06:17.960
into a tiny area 

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:20.213
using lenses?

00:06:20.213 --> 00:06:22.298
By placing light-blocking masks

00:06:22.298 --> 00:06:23.633
on the microscope

00:06:23.633 --> 00:06:24.842
and then shining light through it

00:06:24.842 --> 00:06:27.011
we could project a miniature version of the same graphs 

00:06:27.011 --> 00:06:29.347
onto a tiny photoresist layer

00:06:29.347 --> 00:06:30.848
The mask that controls

00:06:30.848 --> 00:06:32.892
how light passes through

00:06:32.892 --> 00:06:35.269
is now commonly known as a “reticle.”

00:06:35.269 --> 00:06:36.145
Simply put

00:06:36.145 --> 00:06:38.815
this method replaces the material-protecting wax

00:06:38.815 --> 00:06:39.982
with photoresist

00:06:39.982 --> 00:06:41.609
And the way to etch photoresist

00:06:41.609 --> 00:06:42.819
is using reticles

00:06:42.819 --> 00:06:44.862
and a reversed microscope lens

00:06:44.862 --> 00:06:46.781
to ”expose” the photoresist

00:06:46.781 --> 00:06:48.950
Allowing the inherent properties of the photoresist

00:06:48.991 --> 00:06:51.160
to achieve the effect of etching

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:53.579
Lathrop named this light-printing method

00:06:53.579 --> 00:06:55.415
photolithography

00:06:55.415 --> 00:06:57.166
a combination of the words
:

00:06:57.166 --> 00:06:59.210
“photo”

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:00.420
and “lithography”

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:01.546
Photolithography technology

00:07:01.546 --> 00:07:04.006
quickly revolutionized the semiconductor industry

00:07:04.006 --> 00:07:05.675
This process not only improved the precision

00:07:05.675 --> 00:07:07.677
of transistor manufacturing

00:07:07.677 --> 00:07:09.429
but the introduction of reticles

00:07:09.429 --> 00:07:10.847
enabled the high-quality,,large-scale

00:07:10.847 --> 00:07:12.473
production of transistors

00:07:12.473 --> 00:07:14.350
Most importantly, photolithography

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:15.935
paved the way for 

00:07:15.935 --> 00:07:18.479
future chip miniaturization

00:07:18.479 --> 00:07:19.439
In 1957

00:07:19.439 --> 00:07:21.899
Lathrop and Null applied for a patent

00:07:21.899 --> 00:07:24.152
They were publicly honored by the U.S. Army

00:07:24.193 --> 00:07:26.779
and awarded $25,000  bonus

00:07:26.779 --> 00:07:28.156
Looking back now

00:07:28.156 --> 00:07:29.615
we know that the true value

00:07:29.615 --> 00:07:31.868
of this invention far exceeds $25,000

00:07:31.868 --> 00:07:33.286
TI and Fairchild Semiconductor

00:07:33.286 --> 00:07:34.370
at that time

00:07:34.370 --> 00:07:35.580
like many other companies

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:37.039
were both aware of this

00:07:37.039 --> 00:07:39.417
Thus, the race for photolithography began

00:07:43.045 --> 00:07:45.465
Lathrop and Noyce of Fairchild Semiconductors

00:07:45.465 --> 00:07:47.258
were classmates pursuing their doctorates in physics 

00:07:47.258 --> 00:07:49.427
at MIT

00:07:49.427 --> 00:07:51.804
They kept in touch after graduation

00:07:51.804 --> 00:07:53.389
When Noyce heard that

00:07:53.389 --> 00:07:54.515
Lathrop had joined TI

00:07:54.515 --> 00:07:56.017
He immediately decided to

00:07:56.058 --> 00:07:57.768
recruit Lathrop’s work partner

00:07:57.768 --> 00:07:59.645
Null

00:07:59.645 --> 00:08:02.064
Thus,both TI and Fairchild Semiconductor began

00:08:02.064 --> 00:08:04.150
developing photolithography technology

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:06.360
Besides buying photoresist from Kodak

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:08.654
each company worked hard to develop reticles

00:08:08.654 --> 00:08:10.031
Beyondphotolithography

00:08:10.031 --> 00:08:12.033
both companies led respectively by

00:08:12.033 --> 00:08:13.993
Kilby and Noyce

00:08:13.993 --> 00:08:16.037
succeeded in developing ICs

00:08:16.037 --> 00:08:16.913
We’ve already covered

00:08:16.913 --> 00:08:18.915
this part of story

00:08:18.915 --> 00:08:20.249
As time passed

00:08:20.249 --> 00:08:22.835
transistor size shrank

00:08:22.835 --> 00:08:25.838
from centimeters to millimeters, and eventually to microns

00:08:25.838 --> 00:08:27.048
The importance of photolithography

00:08:27.048 --> 00:08:29.091
grew without decline

00:08:29.091 --> 00:08:31.135
The best makers of photolithography equipment

00:08:31.177 --> 00:08:32.637
were no longer semiconductor R&amp;D companies

00:08:32.637 --> 00:08:34.347
like TI

00:08:34.347 --> 00:08:37.183
but rather those with expertise in precision optics

00:08:37.183 --> 00:08:38.684
For example in the 1970s

00:08:38.684 --> 00:08:41.145
the dominant photolithography equipment

00:08:41.145 --> 00:08:42.855
manufacturer that still exists today 

00:08:42.855 --> 00:08:44.148
was an American company

00:08:44.148 --> 00:08:46.484
called Perkin-Elmer

00:08:46.484 --> 00:08:47.985
Perkin-Eimer was skilled in producing

00:08:47.985 --> 00:08:49.612
military-grade sights

00:08:49.612 --> 00:08:52.198
and lenses for recon satellites

00:08:52.198 --> 00:08:54.909
Their ability to precisely align light to patterns  

00:08:54.909 --> 00:08:57.078
let them to quickly dominate the photolithography market

00:08:57.078 --> 00:08:58.579
But in 1980

00:08:58.579 --> 00:09:00.831
the industry leader changed

00:09:00.831 --> 00:09:01.666
Following a suggestion from Morris Chang

00:09:01.666 --> 00:09:04.377
who was still at TI

00:09:04.418 --> 00:09:06.546
A company called GCA

00:09:06.546 --> 00:09:08.089
introduced the world’s first

00:09:08.089 --> 00:09:09.632
stepper

00:09:09.632 --> 00:09:11.801
This stepper could move grid by grid

00:09:11.801 --> 00:09:13.386
across wafers

00:09:13.386 --> 00:09:14.845
printing out pieces of chips

00:09:14.845 --> 00:09:16.681
with identical graphs

00:09:16.681 --> 00:09:18.766
But soon after that, GCA nearly went bankrupt

00:09:18.766 --> 00:09:20.309
due to a strategic misstep:

00:09:20.309 --> 00:09:22.603
overexpansion

00:09:22.603 --> 00:09:23.312
Eventually

00:09:23.312 --> 00:09:25.982
Nikon who had been supplying lenses to GCA

00:09:25.982 --> 00:09:27.650
decided to go its own way

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:30.027
They dismantled and studied GCA’s stepper

00:09:30.027 --> 00:09:32.321
then developed their own

00:09:32.321 --> 00:09:33.739
GCA’s downfall

00:09:33.739 --> 00:09:35.408
coupled with

00:09:35.408 --> 00:09:36.951
Nikon’s superior quality

00:09:36.951 --> 00:09:38.619
and low failure rate

00:09:38.619 --> 00:09:41.706
allowedNikon to surpass GCA in the market

00:09:41.706 --> 00:09:43.874
Japanese companies Nikon and Canon

00:09:43.874 --> 00:09:45.751
had become major competitors

00:09:45.751 --> 00:09:47.545
in photolithography equipment industry

00:09:47.545 --> 00:09:49.630
As the saying goes, ‘Every dog has his day’

00:09:49.630 --> 00:09:52.758
From TI, Perkin Elmer, GCA

00:09:52.758 --> 00:09:55.344
to Nikon and Canon in the 1980s

00:09:55.386 --> 00:09:57.096
the leader in photolithography equipment 

00:09:57.096 --> 00:09:58.556
changed frequently

00:09:58.556 --> 00:10:00.516
A decade later, in the 1990s

00:10:00.516 --> 00:10:03.311
another powerhouse emerged,

00:10:03.311 --> 00:10:04.312
ready to seize control of this critical market

00:10:04.312 --> 00:10:06.230
of photolithography

00:10:06.230 --> 00:10:09.150
The powerhouse was the Dutch manufacturer

00:10:09.150 --> 00:10:10.318
ASML

00:10:10.359 --> 00:10:11.277
The debut of ASML

00:10:11.319 --> 00:10:12.945
and its development of EUV machines

00:10:12.945 --> 00:10:14.113
would totally redefine

00:10:14.113 --> 00:10:16.073
the rules of the photolithography market

00:10:16.073 --> 00:10:18.284
something we now frequently see in the news

00:10:18.284 --> 00:10:19.410
But how did ASML

00:10:19.452 --> 00:10:21.078
rise to dominance?

00:10:21.078 --> 00:10:22.246
Why is it the only company

00:10:22.246 --> 00:10:24.081
capable of producing EUV machines?

00:10:24.081 --> 00:10:26.042
Stay tuned to our channel to find out!

00:10:26.042 --> 00:10:27.501
See you next time

